THE GOD WHO CANNOT DIE
A Story of Draven and Athos
PART ONE: THE WEAPON
Draven did not remember being born.

He remembered waking.

He was standing in a void. Not empty. Not full. Just nothing. And in that nothing, there was a voice.

"You are the End of Gods."

He did not know what that meant. He did not know what he was. He did not know anything.

But he knew the voice. It was not loud. It was not soft. It was simply there. Like gravity. Like time. Like the space between heartbeats.

"Who are you?" Draven asked.

"I am the one who made you."

He was created. He was forged. He was shaped. The Void King poured everything into him. Power. Will. Purpose. The ability to end gods. The ability to end anything.

He gave him a name. Draven. He gave him a purpose. The End of Gods. He gave him a weapon. Himself.

But he also gave him something else. Something he did not mean to give.

He gave him a heart.

Draven did not know this. He did not know he had a heart. He did not know he could feel. He was a weapon. Weapons do not feel.

But he did.

The first god he killed was named Taranis. God of thunder. God of storms. God of a realm that no longer exists.

Draven did not hate him. He did not love him. He did not know him. He simply ended him.

Taranis fought. He threw lightning. He summoned storms. He screamed his rage at the sky. It did not matter. Draven walked through it. He reached out. He ended him.

The realm fell. The people wept. The story closed.

Draven walked away.

He did not feel anything.

But something felt wrong.

PART TWO: THE KILLING
He killed more gods. Many more. He stopped counting after the first hundred.

He killed a god of war. He killed a god of death. He killed a god of time. He killed a god of love. He killed a god of nothing.

Each one was different. Each one was the same. They fought. They begged. They cursed. They died.

Draven did not feel anything.

But something felt wrong.

He started to dream. He did not know he could dream. Weapons do not dream.

He saw a face. A voice. A hand reaching for him. He did not know who it was. He did not know what it meant. But it was there. Every night. Waiting. Watching.

He did not tell the Void King. He did not tell anyone. He did not know how.

He just kept killing.

He walked through Asgard.

He saw Thor's arrogance. The god of thunder believed he could not die. He believed he was invincible. He believed he was the greatest warrior in all the realms.

Draven ended him in seconds.

He saw Odin's weariness. The All-Father had seen too much. He had lived too long. He had lost too much. He did not fight. He simply looked at Draven with his one eye and said:

"I knew you would come."

"You did?"

"I have seen it. In every future. In every possibility. You are the end. You are always the end."

"Then why didn't you stop me?"

"Because I could not. Because no one can. Because you are not a god. You are not a man. You are not anything. You are the end."

Draven ended him.

He did not feel anything.

But something felt wrong.

He walked through Olympus.

He stood before Zeus's throne. The king of the gods trembled. He threw lightning. He summoned storms. He called for his brothers. He called for his children. He called for anyone who would listen.

No one came.

"You cannot kill me," Zeus said. "I am the king of the gods. I am eternal."

"No," Draven said. "You are a story. And I end stories."

He ended him.

Olympus fell. The gods scattered. The myths died.

Draven walked away.

He did not feel anything.

But something felt wrong.

PART THREE: THE VOID
He returned to the void. The place where he was made. The place where he began.

The Void King was waiting.

"You have done well," he said. "You have ended the gods. You have closed the stories. You have brought peace."

"Peace?"

"The gods were arrogant. They were cruel. They were dangerous. You have stopped them. You have saved the multiverse."

"Saved it?"

"Yes. Saved it."

Draven did not speak. He did not know what to say. He did not know what to feel.

He looked at the Void King. The one who made him. The one who gave him purpose. The one who called him son.

"Who am I?" Draven asked.

"You are my weapon."

"No. Who am I?"

"You are the End of Gods."

"No. Who am I?"

The Void King was silent.

"I don't know," he said. "I don't know who you are. I only know what you are."

Draven did not speak.

He walked away.

PART FOUR: THE WANDERING
He wandered. Through realms. Through stories. Through endings.

He did not know where he was going. He did not know what he was looking for. He only knew he could not stay still.

He walked through the realms of the forgotten. The gods no one worships anymore. The stories no one tells. The endings no one remembers.

He ended them. One by one. Realm by realm. Story by story.

He did not feel anything.

But something felt wrong.

He started to remember. Not much. Flashes. Fragments. A face. A voice. A hand reaching for him.

He did not know who it was. He did not know what it meant. But it was there. Every night. Every dream. Waiting. Watching.

He did not tell anyone. He did not know how.

He just kept wandering.

PART FIVE: THE DREAM
One night, he dreamed.

He was not in a realm. He was not in a void. He was in a place he did not recognize.

There was a figure. Tall. Broad. Immovable. Like a mountain.

The figure turned. He had no hair. His eyes were pale gold. Ancient. Empty.

"You are Draven," the figure said.

"Yes."

"You are the End of Gods."

"Yes."

"You are tired."

"Yes."

"You want to stop."

"Yes."

"Then stop."

"I can't."

"Why?"

"Because I don't know how."

The figure was silent. Then he said:

"I am Athos. I am the God Who Cannot Die. I have been waiting for you."

"Waiting for me?"

"Yes. You are the only one who can end me. The only one who can give me what I want."

"What do you want?"

"Death."

Draven woke.

PART SIX: THE MEETING
He found Athos in the edge of everything. The place where reality is thin. The place where stories end.

Athos was waiting.

He was tall. Broad. Immovable. Like a mountain. He had no hair. His eyes were pale gold. Ancient. Empty.

He wore nothing. No armor. No clothes. Just a simple cloth wrapped around his waist.

"You came," Athos said.

"You called."

"No. I waited. You came."

"Why?"

"Because you want to know."

"Know what?"

"Who you are."

Draven was silent.

"I have been here since before the first god," Athos said. "I have watched them rise. I have watched them fall. I have watched them become arrogant. Cruel. Dangerous. I knew they had to be stopped."

"So you made me."

"No. I did not make you. The Void King made you."

"Then what did you do?"

"I waited. I waited for you to come. I waited for you to end me."

"Why?"

"Because I cannot die. I have tried. I have tried everything. Nothing works. I am immortal. I am eternal. I am trapped."

"Trapped?"

"Yes. Trapped in existence. Trapped in time. Trapped in a story that never ends."

Draven did not speak.

"I want to end," Athos said. "I want to feel something. Even if it is death. Can you give me that?"

"I don't know."

"Then we will find out together."

PART SEVEN: THE FIGHT
They fought.

Not a conversation. A battle.

Athos was strong. Immovable. Patient. He did not tire. He did not stop. He did not fall.

Draven was fast. Skilled. The End of Gods. He did not tire. He did not stop. He did not fall.

They were equals. Two immovable objects. Two unstoppable forces. Two ends that could not end each other.

The fight lasted for hours. Days. Weeks. Time meant nothing to them. They were outside it.

Draven struck. Athos took it. He did not fall. He did not bleed. He did not break. He simply stood there.

"Again," Athos said.

Draven attacked again. And again. And again. He used everything. Every technique. Every killing blow.

Athos took it all.

"You have killed gods. I am not a god. You have ended stories. I am not a story. You have ended destinies. I am not a destiny."

"Then what are you?"

"I am the End of Everything. You are the End of Gods. You are the weapon. I am the reason for the weapon."

PART EIGHT: THE TRUTH
Draven stopped fighting.

He looked at Athos. The God Who Cannot Die. The one who had been waiting for him. The one who wanted to end.

"You want to die," Draven said.

"Yes."

"Then let me help you."

"You cannot. You have tried. You have failed."

"No. I have not tried everything."

"What do you mean?"

"You are not a god. You are not a story. You are not a destiny. But you are something."

"What?"

"You are alone."

Athos was silent.

"You have been alone for eons. You have watched everything. You have felt nothing. You want to feel something. Even if it is death."

"Yes."

"Then feel this."

Draven reached out. He put his hand on Athos's shoulder. Not to kill. To touch. To connect.

Athos felt something. Not death. Not pain. Something else.

"What is this?"

"I don't know."

"It hurts."

"I know."

"It feels... good."

"I know."

PART NINE: THE UNDERSTANDING
They stopped fighting.

They stood there. Two beings outside everything. Two ends that could not end each other. Two alone people who had finally found someone who understood.

"You are not a weapon," Athos said.

"I am."

"No. You are a son. The Void King made you to be a weapon. But he gave you a heart. You are not a weapon. You are a person. You are a son."

"I don't know how to be anything else."

"Then learn."

"How?"

"Stay. Stay with me. Let me teach you."

Draven was silent. Then he said:

"I don't know how to stay."

"Then learn."

PART TEN: THE WAITING
Draven did not kill Athos. He could not.

Athos did not kill Draven. He did not want to.

They stood there. Silent. The battle was over. But something else had begun.

Something none of them understood.

"You are not alone," Athos said.

"I am."

"No. You have me. You have yourself. You have the people you have not met yet. You have a future."

"I don't know if I have a future."

"Then make one."

Draven was silent.

"I will wait," Athos said. "I will wait for you. When you are ready. When you know who you are. Come back. I will be here."

"Why?"

"Because you are the only one who can end me. And I am the only one who can teach you. We are the same."

Draven turned and walked away.

He did not know where he was going. He did not know what he was looking for.

But he knew he would find it.

He had to.

EPILOGUE: THE ROAD AHEAD
Draven walked.

He walked through realms. Through stories. Through endings.

He walked until he found a city. A city on the edge of everything. A city called Hallow Bay.

He did not know why he was here. He did not know what he was looking for. But he knew he had to be here.

He stood at the edge of the city. He watched the people. He watched the heroes. He watched the villains.

He felt something. Something he had not felt in eons.

He felt hope.

The story of Draven was not over.

It was just beginning.
